{"kind":"artifact","text":"Documentation set at 212820ca, unmerged on branch task/docs. Closes all three findings from adbefb1d. The P1 was the important one and I had not seen its significance: the push I documented carried a leading plus, which forces. A sequence only advances, so publishing is always a fast-forward and a refused push is git reporting that the remote holds positions this copy has not seen; the forced form removes that refusal and makes overwriting published history an ordinary habit, in a record whose purpose is that a position once taken is final. The refspec is unforced in both the walkthrough and the reference, and the walkthrough now says why, because a reader who does not know why will restore the plus the first time a push is rejected. Verified rather than assumed: a second append publishes as a fast-forward, and a deliberately rewound local ref is rejected by the unforced push. The P2 recovery advice was also wrong — after a failed attach the page said to clone again, but the clone already existed and was never the problem, only the refs were missing; it now says to publish and rerun attach in the same clone, and I ran exactly that sequence end to end, reproducing the documented failure, fixing it with the push, and attaching without touching the clone. The non-blocking finding was correct too: the PATH check discarded its own output and could not fail, so it proved nothing to a reader following along, and command -v gs answers the question actually being asked. 29 links and anchors resolve; make vet, make test, make build, and make ui pass. Not reviewed at this head and not merged.","body":{"commit":"212820caa96269ee98565dd35f39d29188f35563","path":"."}}